Each line shows the newest entry's timestamp for that log. An error message appears if your account can't read a log, instead of a false "NONE".

## How to read it

| Log group | Healthy result |
|---|---|
| Busy: `backend_apache_access`, `backend_pm2_out`, `frontend_apache_access`, `syslog`, `requests`, `compute.googleapis.com/healthchecks` | timestamp within the last few minutes |
| Quiet: the `*_error` and `*_server_error` logs, `*_auth_log`, `frontend_pm2_*`, `cloudbuild`, audit `activity`/`system_event`/`policy`, `postgres.log`, firewall, NAT, `instance_group_manager_events`, `shielded_vm_integrity` | any timestamp is fine. They only write when something happens |
| `data_access` | a timestamp if Data Access logging is on and you can read it. A permission error means you can't see it |
| `vpc_flows` | `NONE`, because flow logs are off |

A busy log with `NONE` or an old timestamp means it isn't arriving.

